<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/6th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Pelusium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/525-bce">525 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelusium">Pelusium</a> was fought in 525 BCE at the eastern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>, where the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-iii">Pharaoh Psamtik III</a>. The victory completed the conquests begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>. It brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, where it was ruled as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twenty-seventh-dynasty-of-egypt">27th Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelusium.jpg" alt="Battle of Pelusium" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Charles Seppings Wright · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_Charles_Seppings-Wright%CB%90_The_Sea_Fight_at_Pelusium_(Hutchinson%27s_History_of_the_Nations,_1922).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Most accounts of the war come from Greek historians, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>. He tells that Pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasis-ii">Amasis II</a>, asked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> for a daughter in marriage, sent instead Nitetis, a daughter of the former pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apries">Apries</a>, and that Cambyses vowed revenge when she revealed the deceit. Herodotus also names Phanes of Halicarnassus, a former adviser of Amasis who escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and helped shape the king's resolve. The King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, an enemy of Amasis, granted Cambyses safe passage on the desert road from Gaza to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelusium">Pelusium</a> and supplied troops. Gaza, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, alone resisted, and fell after a long siege.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Amasis died six months before Cambyses reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-iii">Psamtik III</a> gathered the army. His hope of Greek allies failed, as the Cypriot towns and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates of Samos</a>, who sent 40 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a>, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>. Herodotus says Psamtik had the sons of Phanes killed and their blood mixed with wine and drunk before the battle. Near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelusium">Pelusium</a>, an important city on the eastern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> 30 km south-east of modern Port Said, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> were routed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a> puts the Egyptian dead at fifty thousand and the Persian at seven thousand. The survivors fled to Memphis and were besieged there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> at Memphis killed the crew of a ship sent with a Persian herald, and after the siege two thousand Egyptians died, ten for every Mytilenean killed. Psamtik was captured and allowed to live under watch, but later killed himself after attempting a revolt. Herodotus says Cambyses then had the mummy of Amasis burned, though the historian Pierre Briant judges this account false. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polyaenus">Polyaenus</a>, writing about 700 years later, told the story that Persian soldiers carried sacred animals such as cats before them. Herodotus saw the bones of the dead still lying on the field.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Pelusium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Cambyses II defeated Pharaoh Psamtik III at the <b>Battle of Pelusium</b> in 525 BCE and incorporated Egypt, where the Persian kings count as its 27th Dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
